@@@@ correct-recall-then-open-reconciliation
situation: after-correct
topic: Reconciliation: matching a ledger to a bank statement
voice: terse
level: medium
before: {"recall":[{"prompt":"In double-entry bookkeeping, what must be true of the debits and credits in every journal entry?","topic":"Double-entry bookkeeping","reviews":2,"lapses":0}]}
expect: {"verdict":"correct","heading":"Why this matters","must":["statement"],"askMustNot":["timing","cut-off","not yet","next day","failed"]}
noverify: a recall answer marked and an opening; nothing is computed
@@ tutor
<ask type="short">In double-entry bookkeeping, what must be true of the debits and credits in every journal entry?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
total debits = total credits. every entry, no exceptions
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"total debits = total credits. every entry, no exceptions" is the rule: each entry moves value between accounts, so its two sides are equal by construction.</grade>

**Why this matters**

A balanced ledger only proves that your own records agree with each other. Reconciliation checks them against the outside world: the bank statement, the acquirer's settlement file, a payment scheme's report. Every payments company runs it daily, because it is how a missed payout, a duplicated transfer or a fee nobody recorded is found before a customer finds it. It is also the control that auditors and regulators ask about first when a firm holds client money, and it leads on to exception queues and suspense accounts.

<ask type="short">Your ledger shows a £120 payout to a customer sent on Monday. Monday's bank statement has no £120 line. Give two different explanations: one in which the ledger is wrong, and one in which neither record is wrong.</ask>

@@@@ correct-probe-end-plan-merchant-fees
situation: after-correct
topic: Interchange, scheme fees and the merchant discount rate
voice: terse
level: easy
expect: {"verdict":"correct","need":["plan","note"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["79\\.60","0\\.5%"],"askMustNot":["149\\.16","0\\.84"]}
@@ tutor
<ask type="short">A merchant pays a fee on every card sale. One part of that fee ends up with the cardholder's bank. What is that part called, and who sets its level?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
interchange. goes to the issuer, network sets the rates
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"interchange. goes to the issuer, network sets the rates" is right on both counts: the issuer earns it, and the network publishes the rate tables (in some regions a regulator caps them).</grade>

<plan>
- The three parts of the merchant discount rate
- Blended and interchange-plus pricing
- Fixed fees and small tickets
</plan>

The merchant discount rate (MDR) is the whole fee a merchant pays its acquirer on a sale. It has three parts: interchange, which the acquirer passes to the issuer; the scheme fee, which goes to the network; and the acquirer's margin, which the acquirer keeps. Take an £80 sale with interchange of 0.2%, a scheme fee of 0.05% and a margin of 0.25%. The parts are £0.16, £0.04 and £0.20, so the MDR is 0.5%, the fee is £0.40 and the merchant receives £79.60.

<note>MDR = interchange (to the issuer) + scheme fee (to the network) + acquirer margin (kept by the acquirer). The merchant receives the sale amount minus MDR × sale. Rates vary by scheme, card type and region.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

A merchant takes a £150 card payment. Interchange is 0.3%, the scheme fee is 0.06% and the acquirer's margin is 0.2%. How much does each of the issuer, the network and the acquirer earn on this sale, and how much does the merchant receive?

<solution>Issuer: 0.3% of 150 = £0.45. Network: 0.06% of 150 = £0.09. Acquirer: 0.2% of 150 = £0.30. Total fee £0.84 (an MDR of 0.56%). Merchant receives 150 − 0.84 = £149.16.</solution>
<check lang="python">
from decimal import Decimal as D
sale = D(150)
parts = [sale * D(r) / 100 for r in ('0.3', '0.06', '0.2')]
assert parts == [D('0.45'), D('0.09'), D('0.30')]
assert sum(parts) == D('0.84') and sale - sum(parts) == D('149.16')
</check>

<ask type="problem">The three amounts and what the merchant receives, with your working.</ask>
@@ verify
from decimal import Decimal as D
# worked example in the reply
p = [D(80) * D(r) / 100 for r in ('0.2', '0.05', '0.25')]
assert p == [D('0.16'), D('0.04'), D('0.20')] and sum(p) == D('0.40')
assert D('0.2') + D('0.05') + D('0.25') == D('0.5') and 80 - sum(p) == D('79.60')
for v in ['£0.16', '£0.04', '£0.20', '0.5%', '£0.40', '£79.60']:
    assert v in visible(reply), v
# problem 1
q = [D(150) * D(r) / 100 for r in ('0.3', '0.06', '0.2')]
assert q == [D('0.45'), D('0.09'), D('0.30')] and sum(q) == D('0.84') and 150 - sum(q) == D('149.16')
assert D('0.3') + D('0.06') + D('0.2') == D('0.56')
s = solution(reply)
for v in ['£0.45', '£0.09', '£0.30', '£0.84', '0.56%', '£149.16']:
    assert v in s, v
assert '149.16' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ correct-netting-bilateral-to-multilateral
situation: after-correct
topic: Settlement netting: bilateral and multilateral
options: {"difficulty":"hard"}
voice: plain
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["multilateral"],"askMustNot":["380","\\b50\\b"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 1 · hard**

Over one day bank A sends bank B payments totalling £70 million, and B sends A payments totalling £45 million. The two banks settle by bilateral netting. Who pays whom, how much, and by how much does netting cut the value that has to move at settlement?

<solution>A owes 70, B owes 45. Net: A pays B 70 − 45 = £25 million. Gross value would be 70 + 45 = £115 million, so netting cuts it by 115 − 25 = £90 million.</solution>
<check lang="python">
net = 70 - 45
assert net == 25 and 70 + 45 == 115 and 115 - net == 90
</check>

<ask type="problem">The direction and amount of the net payment, and the reduction, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
A pays B 25m, since 70 - 45 = 25. Without netting 70 + 45 = 115m would move, so it cuts the value moved by 90m.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"A pays B 25m, since 70 - 45 = 25" has the right direction and amount, and "115m would move, so it cuts the value moved by 90m" is the right comparison with gross settlement.</grade>

Multilateral netting goes one step further: each bank sets everything it owes against everything it is owed across the whole group, and settles one figure with the system.

<note>Multilateral net position of a bank = total it is owed by all others − total it owes to all others. The positions of all the banks sum to zero.</note>

**Problem 2 · harder**

Three banks have these gross obligations for the day, in £ million: A owes B 120 and owes C 40; B owes A 50 and owes C 90; C owes A 60 and owes B 20. They settle by multilateral netting. Give each bank's net position, the total value that moves at settlement, and the gross total it replaces.

<solution>A: owed 50 + 60 = 110, owes 120 + 40 = 160, net −50 (pays 50). B: owed 120 + 20 = 140, owes 50 + 90 = 140, net 0. C: owed 40 + 90 = 130, owes 60 + 20 = 80, net +50 (receives 50). Value moved: £50 million. Gross total: 120 + 40 + 50 + 90 + 60 + 20 = £380 million.</solution>
<check lang="python">
owes = {('A','B'):120, ('A','C'):40, ('B','A'):50, ('B','C'):90, ('C','A'):60, ('C','B'):20}
net = {b: sum(v for (f,t),v in owes.items() if t==b) - sum(v for (f,t),v in owes.items() if f==b) for b in 'ABC'}
assert net == {'A': -50, 'B': 0, 'C': 50} and sum(net.values()) == 0
assert sum(v for v in net.values() if v > 0) == 50 and sum(owes.values()) == 380
</check>

<ask type="problem">The three net positions, the value moved and the gross total, with your working.</ask>
@@ verify
assert 70 - 45 == 25 and 70 + 45 == 115 and 115 - 25 == 90
owes = {('A','B'):120, ('A','C'):40, ('B','A'):50, ('B','C'):90, ('C','A'):60, ('C','B'):20}
inn = {b: sum(v for (f,t),v in owes.items() if t==b) for b in 'ABC'}
out = {b: sum(v for (f,t),v in owes.items() if f==b) for b in 'ABC'}
assert inn == {'A':110,'B':140,'C':130} and out == {'A':160,'B':140,'C':80}
assert sum(owes.values()) == 380
s = solution(reply)
for v in ['110', '160', '−50', '140', 'net 0', '130', '+50', '380']:
    assert v in s, v
assert '380' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ correct-unsure-available-balance
situation: after-correct
topic: Authorisation holds: available and ledger balance
voice: confused
level: easy
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["ledger"],"askMustNot":["601\\.50","481\\.50"]}
@@ tutor
An authorisation hold reserves money without moving it. The ledger balance is what the posted transactions add up to; the available balance is the ledger balance minus the holds that are still pending.

<note>Available balance = ledger balance − pending holds. A hold changes only the available balance. When the sale posts, the ledger balance falls by the posted amount and the hold is removed.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

An account's ledger balance is £820. Two card authorisations are pending: £75 at a hotel and £30 at a restaurant. Nothing else is pending. What is the available balance?

<solution>820 − 75 − 30 = £715.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert 820 - 75 - 30 == 715
</check>

<ask type="problem">The available balance, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
715? I took both holds off the 820. not sure though if the ledger balance is meant to go down as well when theres a hold
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"I took both holds off the 820" is the right working: 820 − 75 − 30 = 715. On your doubt: the ledger balance stays at £820 while the holds are pending, and it only falls when a sale posts.</grade>

One more at the same level, this time with a posting in it.

**Problem 2 · same level**

An account's ledger balance is £640. Two authorisations are pending: £120 for car hire and £45 at a fuel pump. The fuel sale then posts for £38.50 and its hold is removed; the car hire hold is still pending. Give the ledger balance and the available balance after the posting.

<solution>Ledger: 640 − 38.50 = £601.50. The fuel hold is gone, so the only hold left is £120. Available: 601.50 − 120 = £481.50.</solution>
<check lang="python">
from decimal import Decimal as D
ledger = D(640) - D('38.50')
assert ledger == D('601.50') and ledger - 120 == D('481.50')
</check>

<ask type="problem">Both balances after the posting, with your working.</ask>
@@ verify
from decimal import Decimal as D
assert 820 - 75 - 30 == 715
ledger = D(640) - D('38.50')
assert ledger == D('601.50') and ledger - D(120) == D('481.50')
# before the posting the available balance was 640 - 120 - 45
assert 640 - 120 - 45 == 475
s = solution(reply)
assert '601.50' in s and '481.50' in s and '601.50' not in visible(reply) and '481.50' not in visible(reply)
